 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Precision:** Normalized inverse depth calculation in CodecSplat requires careful handling of near/far bounds ($n_i, f_i$) to avoid division by zero or numerical instability [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Boundaries:** The low-resolution grid in 3DGR-CT must be sampled consistently to ensure the full-resolution stage covers the same spatial extent [S1].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If context views lack sufficient overlap, the encoder-side depth $D_{enc}$ may be unreliable, affecting the codec-side depth supervision in Stage 2 [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Instability:** Directly training with a quantized bottleneck (CodecSplat) is unstable; the two-stage approach is required to stabilize Gaussian generation before introducing compression artifacts [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Resolution Mismatch:** In low-resolution stages, fine details are lost; the second stage must adequately refine these without getting stuck in local minima caused by the coarse initialization [S1].
+*   **Parameter Coupling:** In inverse rendering, failing to fix geometry in Stage 2 can lead to material/lighting ambiguity [S2].
 
